#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y = f(2x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,6-1) = (1,5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a80933c-10f7-51d8-9b1a-039e3550e2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158540" :question/problem "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6d8898-251c-5832-a715-c1e0b2d67196" :question/math-academy-id "q-158542" :question/problem "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "626e7d97-1fd4-5fea-b684-7fc6a67422d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-211287" :question/problem "![](images/28/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb982b3-78f3-5bb2-88d8-08e1c9cc7f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288576} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b214ed-c977-5da8-9b98-583c8599dbc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289314} {:answer/id #uuid "cc01c367-7b62-52ca-b3f4-660a5da6d436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289386} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cb2914-db63-5a0a-b325-34eb1a5b726a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3166ba-184a-5116-9d35-4f34e933136f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2903fa-7395-523d-a538-0dfcbb4ba792" :question/math-academy-id "q-211288" :question/problem "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$"}]}
